# Exercise: Predicates
#
# A predicate is a word that returns a Bool. For an ADT, the most
# important kind of predicate asks "which constructor produced this
# value?" — so we can branch on it with `if`.
#
# Two primitives drive this:
#
#   variant.tag    ( Variant -- Symbol )
#       returns the symbol that labelled the variant when it was
#       constructed (e.g. :Empty or :Cons).
#
#   symbol.=       ( Symbol Symbol -- Bool )
#       compares two symbols for equality.
#
# Lisp lineage: `empty?` is what Lisp calls `null?`. We only build
# one predicate here; the other (`cons?`) is just `empty? not`, so
# defining both is busywork.
#
# Your task: Define
#
#   empty? ( List -- Bool )
#
# Returns true if the list was built by `empty`, false if it was
# built by `prepend`.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# ─── Already defined for you (from 01) ─────────────────────────
: empty ( -- Variant )         :Empty variant.make-0 ;
: prepend ( T Variant -- Variant ) :Cons variant.make-2 ;
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

: empty? ( Variant -- Bool )
    # Your code here — check the variant's tag against :Empty.
    drop false
;

# Do not edit below this line

: test-empty-is-empty ( -- )
    empty empty? test.assert
;

: test-cons-is-not-empty ( -- )
    empty 1 swap prepend empty? not test.assert
;
